    "### 6. The correlation is small and negative. Explain in two sentences what in the shape of this density makes 𝑋 and 𝑌 pull against each other."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a1da76d4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The density $f_{XY}(x, y) = x + y$ grows linearly as either x or y increases, and thus it lowers the probability mass near (0, 0) where both variables would be small together, while keeping high, uniform contour bands along the anti-diagonal lines $x + y = c$ where one variable is large and the other is small. As a result, when $X$ is small, $Y$ is strongly pushed toward larger values to keep $x + y$ large, causing the two variables to slightly pull against each other on average (which can be better seen in the heat map)."
   ]
