    "### Part 5: \n",
    "\n",
    "The estimator with the smallest mean squared error among *all* functions of $X$, linear or not, is the\n",
    "conditional mean $E[Y\\mid X]$. For jointly Gaussian data, Part 1 shows this conditional mean is\n",
    "$0.5\\,x$, which is already a linear function of $x$. So the best linear estimator and the best possible\n",
    "estimator coincide, and no nonlinear function can beat the linear one. For non-Gaussian data this fails. In\n",
    "Step 2, $Y=X^2$ is perfectly predictable from $X$ by a nonlinear function while the best linear\n",
    "estimator is useless, because the correlation is zero. Because the conditional variance is also constant, the\n",
    "achieved error is the same for every observed $x$. It equals $\\Sigma_{a|b}=\\Sigma_{aa}-\\Sigma_{ab}\\Sigma_{bb}^{-1}\\Sigma_{ba}$,\n",
    "which is exactly the MMSE expression $\\sigma_s^2-\\Sigma_{sx}^T\\Sigma_{xx}^{-1}\\Sigma_{sx}$ we use in Step 8."
   ]
